+# US-20: Training speed target
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Epic | WBMME-2 Model Training & Selection |
+| Requirement | MLE-NF-002 |
+| Points / sprint | 3 / Sprint 2 |
+| Owner | Varnika (Member 2) |
+
+**Story.** As a Student I want training to finish quickly on a normal-sized dataset, so that I can try several models in one lab session.
+
+## Requirement
+
+With a 50,000-row, 20-column dataset, each model in the catalogue trains with its **default** settings in **60 seconds or less**.
+
+## How it is measured
+
+`training/benchmarks/speed.py` builds a fixed synthetic dataset (15 numeric columns, 3 with 5% missing values, 5 text columns with 8 values each, 3-class target, seed 0), then times `pipeline.train` for every catalogue model. The timer covers reading the CSV, preparation, the split and the fit, which is the same code a real job runs.
+
+```
+python -I training/benchmarks/speed.py          # prints a JSON report, exit code 1 if any model is over the limit
+python -I -m pytest -c member2_dev/pytest.ini -m perf
+```
+
+## Result (2 CPUs, Python 3.13, Linux)
+
+| Model | Before fix | After fix | Limit |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Logistic regression | n/a | 1.8 s | 60 s |
+| k-Nearest Neighbours | n/a | 0.5 s | 60 s |
+| Decision tree | n/a | 1.5 s | 60 s |
+| Random forest (100 trees, 1 core) | **67.6 s (failed)** | 12.9 s | 60 s |
+
+Raw data: `docs/member2/reports/training_speed_result.json`.
+
+## Defect found and fixed
+
+The first run failed: the random forest took 67.6 s. Profiling showed that the preparation step returned a **sparse** matrix, which scikit-learn's tree models process much more slowly (66 s sparse against 12 s dense for the same data). `training/pipeline.py` now produces dense features by default. Only a table wide enough to need more than about 480 MB of dense data falls back to sparse, to protect server memory (`MAX_DENSE_CELLS`).
+
+## Notes for the viva
+
+- The limit is machine dependent. The result above was measured on a small 2-CPU container, so a typical laptop should be faster. The benchmark is kept as a script so the team can re-measure on their own machine.
+- The random forest keeps `n_jobs=1` (Table 4.3-1) so that one user's job cannot take every core of a shared server.
